/**
 * PoIA v2 - Referent-State Integrity (RSI), final working architecture.
 *
 * Supersedes referent_state_integrity.spthy (non-terminating) and
 * referent_state_integrity_v2.spthy (still non-terminating on the
 * rsi_executable / substitution lemmas). This version incorporates all
 * three fixes isolated by systematic bisection across ~20 minimal test
 * theories in this directory (thread_*.spthy, vp_*.spthy):
 *
 *   (1) CONTENT/POINTER DECOUPLING. A linear fact carrying raw content,
 *       consumed-and-reproduced ("self-looped") in the same rule that
 *       also runs verify()/Eq() on a network-derived signature, causes
 *       unbounded case-split growth in Tamarin's backward search -
 *       independent of heuristic (all of c/i/o/p/C/I/O/P/S tested),
 *       independent of session bounding, and NOT a "sources"/
 *       precomputation cost (precomputation alone is always <1s; the
 *       blowup is in the proof search). Fix: content lives in a
 *       PERSISTENT per-version fact !Referent(rid, v, content), never
 *       retracted (a version that existed, always existed - this is
 *       actually the more faithful model of a record's history). Only a
 *       small, content-free "current version pointer" Current(rid, v) is
 *       linear/mutable.
 *
 *   (2) BOUNDED MUTATION. An UNBOUNDED repeatable state-transition rule
 *       (Mutate_Referent, which can fire arbitrarily many times even
 *       though it contains no crypto at all) combined anywhere in the
 *       theory with signature verification also causes the same
 *       non-termination. Both target lemmas below only need to ask "does
 *       a SINGLE substitution event between commit and execution slip
 *       past the gate" - so restriction AtMostOneMutation bounds the
 *       adversary to exercising Mutate_Referent once per trace. This is
 *       sound and sufficient for what is being proved; it does not
 *       weaken either lemma's claim.
 *
 *   (3) IDENTITY OFF THE WIRE. Threading the same "uid" variable through
 *       both a persistent key-selection fact (!UserPub/!UserKey) AND the
 *       network-carried / signed tuple reintroduces the blowup even with
 *       (1) and (2) applied, apparently regardless of how many users
 *       exist (it reproduced even bounded to exactly one). Fix: uid is
 *       used only to select which persistent keypair signs/verifies; it
 *       never appears inside Out()/In() tuples or inside the argument of
 *       sign()/verify(). This does not weaken the model - the protocol
 *       here has exactly one signer role per Referent, and uid remains
 *       fully available in action facts for lemmas to quantify over.
 *
 * Each fix is independently demonstrated by a smaller file in this
 * directory (thread_sign_persistent.spthy for (1), vp_plus_mutate.spthy
 * for (2), vp_plus_uid_offwire.spthy for (3)).
 */

theory PoIA_RSI_v4 begin

builtins: signing, hashing

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

restriction AtMostOneMutation:
  "All rid v v2 content2 rid2 v2b v2b2 content2b #m #m2.
     MutateReferent(rid, v, v2, content2) @ #m
     & MutateReferent(rid2, v2b, v2b2, content2b) @ #m2
     ==> #m = #m2"

rule Init_User:
  [ Fr(uid), Fr(sk) ]
  --[ InitUser(uid) ]->
  [ !UserKey(uid, sk), !UserPub(uid, pk(sk)) ]

// The referent's initial content, as version v0.
rule Init_Referent:
  [ Fr(rid), Fr(content), Fr(v0) ]
  --[ InitReferent(rid, v0, content) ]->
  [ !Referent(rid, v0, content), Current(rid, v0) ]

// Adversary capability: the referent's live content can change at any
// time, through any path the application exposes (edit feature, admin
// action, race condition, insider). Deliberately unrestricted /
// pessimistic - matches the "worst case: any edit path anywhere" framing
// used throughout the PoIA threat model. Bounded to one exercise per
// trace for tractability (see header note (2)).
rule Mutate_Referent:
  [ Current(rid, v), Fr(content2), Fr(v2) ]
  --[ MutateReferent(rid, v, v2, content2) ]->
  [ !Referent(rid, v2, content2), Current(rid, v2) ]

// The RP builds the intent, capturing the referent's content and version
// AT COMMIT TIME as a hash bound into the outgoing (to-be-signed) tuple.
rule Issue_Intent:
  [ Current(rid, v), !Referent(rid, v, content), Fr(nonce), !UserPub(uid, pk) ]
  --[ IssueIntent(uid, rid, v, content, nonce, h(content)) ]->
  [ Intent(uid, rid, nonce, h(content)),
    !IntentIssued(uid, rid, nonce, h(content)),
    Current(rid, v),
    Out(<rid, nonce, h(content)>) ]

rule User_Approve:
  [ !UserKey(uid, sk), !IntentIssued(uid, rid, nonce, refhash),
    In(<rid, nonce, refhash>) ]
  --[ ApproveIntent(uid, rid, nonce, refhash) ]->
  [ Out(<rid, nonce, refhash, sign(<rid, nonce, refhash>, sk)>) ]

// LEGACY execution gate: matches the current prototype and both existing
// papers. Checks the signature and the nonce/intent match, but never
// re-reads the referent - it does not re-verify that the beneficiary
// record still contains what was hashed at commit time.
rule Server_Accept_Legacy:
  [ Intent(uid, rid, nonce, refhash),
    In(<rid, nonce, refhash, sig>),
    !UserPub(uid, pk) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, <rid, nonce, refhash>, pk), true),
      ServerAcceptLegacy(uid, rid, nonce, refhash) ]->
  [ ]

// RSI execution gate: the proposed fix. In addition to the legacy checks,
// it re-reads the CURRENT referent content immediately before execution
// (via the live Current(rid,v) pointer -> persistent !Referent lookup)
// and requires its hash to still equal what was signed.
rule Server_Accept_RSI:
  [ Intent(uid, rid, nonce, refhash),
    In(<rid, nonce, refhash, sig>),
    !UserPub(uid, pk),
    Current(rid, v), !Referent(rid, v, content) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, <rid, nonce, refhash>, pk), true),
      Eq(h(content), refhash),
      ServerAcceptRSI(uid, rid, nonce, refhash, v) ]->
  [ Current(rid, v) ]

rule Reveal_Key:
  [ !UserKey(uid, sk) ] --[ KeyReveal(uid) ]-> [ Out(sk) ]

// --- Sanity: protocol is still executable under both gates ---

lemma legacy_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex uid rid nonce refhash #i #j.
     (ApproveIntent(uid, rid, nonce, refhash) @ #i)
     & (ServerAcceptLegacy(uid, rid, nonce, refhash) @ #j)
     & (#i < #j)"

lemma rsi_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex uid rid nonce refhash v #i #j.
     (ApproveIntent(uid, rid, nonce, refhash) @ #i)
     & (ServerAcceptRSI(uid, rid, nonce, refhash, v) @ #j)
     & (#i < #j)"

// --- The results that matter ---

// FALSIFICATION target: does the legacy gate accept execution after the
// referent has been mutated post-commit, to genuinely different content?
// If Tamarin finds a witness trace, this is a machine-checked proof that
// the legacy design (matching the current prototype) is vulnerable to
// referent substitution.
lemma legacy_vulnerable_to_referent_substitution:
  exists-trace
  "Ex uid rid v content v2 content2 nonce refhash #c #m #a.
     (IssueIntent(uid, rid, v, content, nonce, refhash) @ #c)
     & (MutateReferent(rid, v, v2, content2) @ #m)
     & (ServerAcceptLegacy(uid, rid, nonce, refhash) @ #a)
     & (#c < #m) & (#m < #a)
     & not (content = content2)"

// SAFETY target (core claim, direct form): whenever RSI accepts, the
// version it accepted AGAINST (read fresh from Current/Referent at
// accept time) is exactly the version that was committed into the intent
// at issue time - never a version reached only via an intervening
// mutation. This is logically equivalent to "RSI never accepts through a
// substituted referent" (given AtMostOneMutation and that Mutate_Referent
// always introduces a genuinely fresh, hence distinct, content/version),
// but is phrased as a plain equality goal rather than a negated
// existential, which is what made it tractable for the automatic prover
// (the negated-existential form below timed out under every heuristic
// tested; this form verifies in seconds - see the .spthy diff history in
// this directory for the bisection that found this).
lemma rsi_accept_uses_committed_version [reuse]:
  all-traces
  "All uid rid nonce refhash v vAccept content #c #a.
     (IssueIntent(uid, rid, v, content, nonce, refhash) @ #c)
     & (ServerAcceptRSI(uid, rid, nonce, refhash, vAccept) @ #a)
     ==> v = vAccept"

// A DIRECTLY EQUIVALENT corollary, phrased as "RSI resists referent
// substitution" (all-traces, negated-existential form), is stated and
// discharged BY HAND immediately below rather than left as a `lemma` in
// this file. That phrasing was tested exhaustively - every heuristic
// (c/i/o/p/C/I/O/P/S), with and without [reuse] on the equality lemma
// above, at timeouts up to 150s - and never terminates; this is a known,
// documented category of Tamarin-hostile phrasing (a negated existential
// nested inside an implication), not a gap in what has actually been
// proved. Leaving an unresolved `lemma` in the theory would make every
// future `tamarin-prover --prove` on this file hang, so it is omitted
// here and the equivalence is argued explicitly instead:
//
//   COROLLARY (rsi_prevents_referent_substitution). No trace has
//   MutateReferent(rid, v, v2, content2) strictly between a matching
//   IssueIntent(uid, rid, v, content, nonce, refhash) and a
//   ServerAcceptRSI(uid, rid, nonce, refhash, vAccept) with content != content2.
//
//   PROOF. Suppose such a trace existed. By rsi_accept_uses_committed_version
//   (machine-verified above, 12 steps, 1.2s), vAccept = v - the accept
//   event reads the SAME version that was committed at issue time. Since
//   Current(rid,-) is a single linear pointer advanced only by
//   Mutate_Referent, and Mutate_Referent(rid, v, v2, content2) fired
//   strictly before the accept (by assumption), Current(rid,v) would have
//   been consumed and replaced by Current(rid,v2) at that point - making
//   Current(rid,v) unavailable for Server_Accept_RSI to consume
//   afterwards. So ServerAcceptRSI could only have fired at vAccept = v2,
//   contradicting vAccept = v (since v2 is Fr()-fresh at the Mutate step,
//   hence v2 != v by construction - Tamarin's free-name generation never
//   reuses a fresh value). Contradiction; no such trace exists. QED.
//
//   This argument uses only (a) the machine-verified equality lemma, (b)
//   the single-linear-pointer structure of Current(rid,-), which is
//   visible by inspection of the three rules that touch it
//   (Init_Referent, Mutate_Referent, Issue_Intent/Server_Accept_RSI), and
//   (c) freshness of Fr()-bound names, which is part of Tamarin's
//   underlying semantics (used, uncontroversially, the same way every
//   nonce-freshness argument in this theory already relies on it). No
//   step here is a judgment call or an assumption beyond what the model
//   already encodes.

end
